1
00:03:50,909 --> 00:03:56,909
When there’s no more room in HELL, the dead will walk the EARTH!

2
00:07:41,818 --> 00:07:47,818
Dawn of the Dead is a horror, science fiction film directed by George A. Romero.

3
00:11:32,727 --> 00:11:38,727
During an ever-growing epidemic of zombies that have risen from the dead, two Philadelphia SWAT team members, a
traffic reporter, and his television-executive girlfriend seek refuge in a secluded shopping mall.

4
00:15:23,636 --> 00:15:29,636
Stephen "Flyboy" Andrews, played by David Emge.

5
00:19:14,545 --> 00:19:20,545
Peter Washington, played by Ken Foree.

6
00:23:05,454 --> 00:23:11,454
Roger "Trooper" DeMarco, played by Scott H. Reiniger.

7
00:26:56,363 --> 00:27:02,363
Francine "Fran" Parker, played by Gaylen Ross.

8
00:30:47,272 --> 00:30:53,272
Dr. James Foster, played by David Crawford.

9
00:34:38,181 --> 00:34:44,181
Sidney Berman, played by David Early.

10
00:38:29,090 --> 00:38:35,090
Dawn of the Dead  is a 1978 zombie horror film
written, directed, and edited by George A.

11
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:26,000
Romero, and produced by Richard P.

12
00:46:10,909 --> 00:46:16,909
Rubinstein.

13
00:50:01,818 --> 00:50:07,818
An American-Italian international co-production, it is the second film in Romero's series of zombie films, and though it contains no characters or
settings from the preceding film Night of the Living Dead (1968), it shows the larger-scale effects of a zombie apocalypse on society.

14
00:53:52,727 --> 00:53:58,727
In the film, a phenomenon of unidentified origin has caused the
reanimation of the dead, who prey on human flesh.

15
00:57:43,636 --> 00:57:49,636
David Emge, Ken Foree, Scott Reiniger, and Gaylen Ross star as survivors of the outbreak who
barricade themselves inside a suburban shopping mall during mass hysteria.

16
01:01:34,545 --> 01:01:40,545
Romero waited to make another zombie film after Night of the Living Dead for
several years to avoid being stereotyped as a horror director.

17
01:05:25,454 --> 01:05:31,454
Upon visiting Monroeville Mall in Monroeville, Pennsylvania, with a friend whose company managed
the complex, he decided to use the location as the basis for the film's story.

18
01:09:16,363 --> 01:09:22,363
The project came to the attention of Italian filmmaker Dario Argento who, along with his brother Claudio and producer
Alfredo Cuomo, agreed to co-finance the film in exchange for its international distribution rights.

19
01:13:07,272 --> 01:13:13,272
Argento also consulted with Romero during the scriptwriting phase.

20
01:16:58,181 --> 01:17:04,181
Principal photography on Dawn of the Dead took place between November 1977
and February 1978 on location in Monroeville and Pittsburgh.

21
01:20:49,090 --> 01:20:55,090
The special make-up effects were created by Tom Savini, whose work on the film led to
an extensive career creating similar effects for other horror films.

22
01:24:40,000 --> 01:24:46,000
In post-production, Romero and Argento edited separate
versions of the film for their respective markets.

23
01:28:30,909 --> 01:28:36,909
Argento's version features a progressive rock score composed and performed by his frequent collaborators
Goblin, while Romero's cut primarily favors stock cues from the De Wolfe Music Library.

24
01:32:21,818 --> 01:32:27,818
Following its Italian premiere on September 1, 1978, Dawn of the
Dead was released in other markets the following year.

25
01:36:12,727 --> 01:36:18,727
Despite facing difficulties with various national censorship boards – in the United States, it was released unrated to improve its commercial prospects after it was given an X by the Motion Picture Association of America, and in
Britain it was liable for seizure during the 1980s "video nasties" moral panic – the film proved to be a major success at the box office, grossing $66 million worldwide against its estimated budget of $640,000.

26
01:40:03,636 --> 01:40:09,636
Noted for its satirical portrayal of consumerism, Dawn of the Dead has received widespread critical acclaim since its initial
release, and is widely considered to be one of the greatest horror films ever made, as well as the greatest zombie film.

27
01:43:54,545 --> 01:44:00,545
Like its predecessor, it has garnered a large, international cult following.

28
01:47:45,454 --> 01:47:51,454
In 2008, it was chosen by Empire magazine as one of The 500 Greatest
Movies of All Time, along with Night of the Living Dead.

29
01:51:36,363 --> 01:51:42,363
Dawn of the Dead was followed by four official sequels, beginning with 1985's Day of the Dead, and a
separate series of unofficial Italian-made sequels, beginning with 1979's Zombi 2.

30
01:55:27,272 --> 01:55:33,272
It has also inspired a 2004 remake film directed by Zack Snyder, as well as numerous parodies and pop
culture references in other media such as Shaun of the Dead, Dead Rising, and Left 4 Dead.

31
01:59:18,181 --> 01:59:24,181
The film explores themes of helicopter, army, chaos, bite, materialism.

32
02:03:09,090 --> 02:03:15,090
The tone is dark and thought_provoking.
